WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
If you draw the straight line closest to your data and read

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.100
new answers off it, you get linear regression. If you bend

3
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:10.600
that line into an S so it answers yes or no instead of a

4
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:14.100
number, you get logistic regression. If you stop drawing

5
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.850
lines and ask yes-or-no questions until you reach an answer,

6
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:21.350
you get a decision tree. If you grow 100 trees, each on a

7
00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:25.100
random mix of the data, and let them vote on the answer, you

8
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.600
get a random forest. If you grow small trees one by one instead,

9
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.100
each fixing the mistakes

10
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.600
still left, you get gradient boosting. If you skip training

11
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.100
and let the five points closest to a new one vote on its

12
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.350
answer, you get k-nearest neighbours. If you hide the

13
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.850
answers, drop 3 centres, let each point join its nearest

14
00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:47.350
centre, then move each centre to the middle of its group

15
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:50.850
until the groups settle, you get k-means. If you go back to

16
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.350
that S and feed many of them into layer after

17
00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.850
layer, so together they can learn almost any pattern, you

18
00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.600
get a neural network. If you make that network

19
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.600
huge and train it on mountains of text to score every

20
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:08.600
possible next word, you get an LLM. And if you zoom into

21
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.600
that LLM, you get mostly that same line and bend, set by

22
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:13.900
billions of numbers.

